    "summary": "Reno '82 delivers a high‑energy Stranger→Franklin's blaze and killer second set",
    "review": "The March 13, 1982 Reno show is pure early‑'80s fire, especially in the second set where the band rolls straight into a smoking Feel Like A Stranger → Franklin’s Tower that never lets up. Jerry shreds with an almost manic tempo, throws in a slipknot tease at the end of Eyes of the World and delivers poignant vocals on the closing numbers – all backed by Brent Mydland’s electric‑piano depth that feels fully baked into the mix. Phil “drops bombs” on the low end, anchoring those wild jams, while Bob locks in tight rhythm and trades vocal lines with Jerry on the Stranger segues. The drums from Bill (and Mickey) keep a solid, driving pulse through Space, The Other One and the seamless segue into Black Peter, making the whole set feel like one continuous improvisational wave.\n\nIf you’re hunting a clean source, the Miller soundboard recording captures every nuance with crystal‑clear instrument separation and no drop‑outs – it’s the definitive way to experience this high‑octane night. Even the audience tapes are surprisingly vivid, but nothing beats the board’s fidelity for hearing Brent’s “salty gruff” keyboards and Phil’s thunderous bass in full glory.",
